import VaringPackable.Extraction

/-!
# varing — specifications (packing two integers into one wider integer)

Implementation: `extracted/VaringPackable` (verbatim `cargo hax extract
varing-packable` output for al8n/varing@24719329): the module
`varing::packable::builtin` (the `pack_*` / `unpack_*` const fns for the builtin
integer widths and every `Packable` impl among them) and the zigzag helpers
`varing::utils::zigzag_{encode,decode}_i{8,16,32,64}` it calls.

The property tests are the `quickcheck::quickcheck!` blocks generated by the
`impl_packable_for_primitives!` macro in `src/packable/builtin.rs`:

* the `@self_packable` arm (tests at lines 119-143), instantiated for
  `8 => 16, 16 => 32, 32 => 64, 64 => 128` (lines 500-505):
  ```rust
  fn fuzzy_roundtrip_u64(a: u64, b: u64) -> bool {
    let packed = a.pack(&b);
    let (a2, b2) = <u64 as Packable<u64, _>>::unpack(packed);
    a == a2 && b == b2
  }
  ```
  (and likewise `fuzzy_roundtrip_i$bits`, `fuzzy_roundtrip_u$bits_i$bits`,
  `fuzzy_roundtrip_i$bits_u$bits` with the corresponding `Packable` impls);
* the `@exchange` arm (tests at lines 203-248) and the `@mix` arm (tests at
  lines 366-436), instantiated for `(8, 16) => 32, (8, 32) => 64, (8, 64) => 128,
  (16, 32) => 64, (16, 64) => 128, (32, 64) => 128` (lines 507-514):
  ```rust
  fn fuzzy_mix_roundtrip_u32_u64(a: u32, b: u64) -> bool {
    let packed = a.pack(&b);
    let (a2, b2) = <u32 as Packable<u64, _>>::unpack(packed);
    let cond1 = a == a2 && b == b2;

    let packed = b.pack(&a);
    let (a2, b2) = <u64 as Packable<u32, _>>::unpack(packed);
    let cond2 = a == b2 && b == a2;

    cond1 && cond2
  }
  ```

Each spec executes the test body in the extracted semantics: `x.pack(&y)` with
`x : X`, `y : Y` is the statically dispatched impl method
`<X as Packable<Y, P>>::pack`, extracted as `X.Insts.VaringPackablePackableYP.pack x y`
(the `&` borrows of `Copy` integers are passed by value), and
`<X as Packable<Y, _>>::unpack(p)` is `X.Insts.VaringPackablePackableYP.unpack p`.
The returned `bool` of the quickcheck property becomes the postcondition:
`==` on integers is equality of the extracted scalars and `&&` is `∧`
(no call is skipped by the short-circuit: both packings are computed before
the final `cond1 && cond2`).

Generated inputs: quickcheck 1.x `Arbitrary` for the builtin integer types
draws from the full range of the type (a uniform value, or with probability
1/10 one of the "problem values" such as `MIN`, `MAX`, `0`), and the tests have
no `TestResult::discard`, so the theorems have no hypotheses.

Notation: `e ⦃ r => P r ⦄` (Aeneas `WP.spec`) holds iff the Rust computation `e`
terminates without panicking, returning `ok r`, and `P r` holds. Arithmetic in
the extracted code follows Rust debug-build semantics (overflow panics; `<<` /
`>>` panic only when the shift amount is at least the bit width, and `<<`
discards the bits shifted out), which is the configuration `cargo test` runs
the property tests under.
-/

open CoreModels Aeneas Aeneas.Std.WP
open Aeneas.Std hiding namespace core alloc
open RustM

namespace Benchmark.Cases.Varing.Packable

open varing

/-- PBT `packable::builtin::fuzzy_roundtrip_u64` (src/packable/builtin.rs:120-124,
`@self_packable` arm, instance `64 => 128`):
`let packed = a.pack(&b); let (a2, b2) = <u64 as Packable<u64, _>>::unpack(packed);
a == a2 && b == b2`. -/
def roundtrip_u64_spec (a b : Std.U64) : Prop :=
  (do
    let packed ← U64.Insts.VaringPackablePackableU64U128.pack a b
    U64.Insts.VaringPackablePackableU64U128.unpack packed)
  ⦃ r => a = r.1 ∧ b = r.2 ⦄

